.worktrees/

# Backups
.backup-*/

# Dogfood harness workspace. The rendered ASSETS (agents/, commands/, skills/,
# schemas/, rubrics/, stages/, hooks/, settings.json, …) are regenerable from
# templates via /hm:make, so they stay ignored. Only the human-authored,
# NON-regenerable content is tracked: harness.yaml (config) + memory/ (the
# failures/wiki/session tiers wrapup commits). This REPLACES the old blanket
# `.claude/` (added 2026-05-29 in 45538b26 as an apparent side effect), which
# ignored those tracked deliverables too — so `git add .claude/memory/x.md` was
# refused and every NEW memory file was silently dropped by wrapup's
# `git add … || true` (data loss under the per-task worktree model).
#
# Uses `.claude/*` (NOT `.claude/`) so the negations are reachable — git cannot
# re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded (same reason work-docs/*
# is spelled with the trailing `*` below). Verify any edit here with
# `git check-ignore -v .claude/memory/wiki.md` — it must print NOTHING.
.claude/*
!.claude/harness.yaml
!.claude/memory/
# Mutation receipts are EVIDENCE, not churn — the re-include for them lives with the
# observability block further down, NOT here. It was here first, and a concurrent branch
# added its own `.claude/observability/*` block BELOW it; git resolves by LAST match, so
# the negation above that block was dead on arrival while `git check-ignore` still
# answered "not ignored" — because the file was already tracked and check-ignore skips
# tracked paths. `--no-index` is what showed the truth. Two correct edits merging into a
# silently wrong file is the hazard this whole directory's comments are about.
# …but memory churn (LLM working tiers + runtime locks) stays ignored even though
# memory/ is re-included:
.claude/memory/semantic/
.claude/memory/episodic/
.claude/memory/profile/
.claude/memory/*.lock

# Replay corpus (ADR-006 part 2 of PLAN-workflow-loop-efficiency) — the ONLY
# thing under observability/ that is committed.
#
# The detection check for pipeline changes was declared unimplementable three
# times, the last time because no per-reviewer finding payload had ever been
# persisted anywhere. Persisting it into a gitignored path would have repeated
# the mistake in a quieter form: a replay corpus that exists only on the machine
# that produced it cannot verify anyone else's pipeline change, or a change made
# after a fresh clone. Committing it is what makes it an artifact rather than a
# scratch file.
#
# `.claude/observability/` is written LITERALLY here, ahead of the negation, on
# purpose: `worktree._HARNESS_CHURN_DIRS` contains that exact string and
# `_ensure_gitignore_entries` appends any pattern it does not find as an exact
# line. Appended text lands at EOF, after the negation, and git resolves by
# last-match — so an implicit-only exclusion would be re-added below this block
# on the next `worktree create` and would silently re-ignore the corpus. With
# the literal line present, that appender finds it and skips.
#
# Verify BOTH directions after any edit here:
#   git check-ignore -v .claude/observability/review-payloads/x/y.json   -> NOTHING
#   git check-ignore -v .claude/observability/review-2026-01-01.jsonl    -> ignored
# The literal `.claude/observability/` MUST appear as its own line, and must then be
# immediately re-included. `_ensure_gitignore_entries` skips a pattern only on an EXACT
# line match; without the literal it re-derives the pattern as un-covered and appends it at
# EOF, where git's last-match rule beats the negation and re-ignores the corpus. Verified
# the hard way: a manual run of the appender reported no change, and the real test caught
# it appending anyway — so assert with the test, not with a one-off invocation.
.claude/observability/
!.claude/observability/
.claude/observability/*
!.claude/observability/review-payloads/
# Mutation receipts (PLAN-mechanical-guards-from-backlog) — same argument as the corpus
# above, and it MUST stay below `.claude/observability/*` for the same last-match reason.
# Verify with `--no-index`; without it, an already-tracked ledger reports "not ignored"
# no matter what the rules say.
!.claude/observability/mutation-receipts.jsonl

# Cursor IDE workspace
.cursor/

# Codex CLI workspace
.codex/
.agents/
AGENTS.md

# Session work documents (loop context, batch state) — churn only.
# Deliverables are NEGATED below: CLAUDE.md states "Deliverables (PLAN/REVIEW/
# RESEARCH/SPEC, human memory tiers) are deliberately NOT ignored — wrapup
# commits them", and 53 of them are tracked. This blanket rule (added 2026-05-29
# in 45538b26, a `chore: re-render .claude` — apparently a side effect) contradicted
# that: gitignore only affects UNTRACKED files, so the pre-existing 53 kept
# committing normally while every NEW deliverable was silently dropped by wrapup's
# `git add ... || true`. Under the per-task worktree model that is data loss, not
# just churn: the deliverable exists ONLY in `.worktrees/<slug>/`, and `task-land`
# deletes that worktree. Cost one PLAN + RESEARCH + REVIEW on 2026-07-17.
# NOTE the trailing `*`, not `/`: git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT
# DIRECTORY is excluded, so `work-docs/` + `!work-docs/PLAN-*.md` silently does
# nothing (git never descends into an excluded dir). `work-docs/*` excludes the
# CONTENTS, leaving the negations reachable. Verify any edit here with
# `git check-ignore -v work-docs/PLAN-x.md` — it must print NOTHING.
work-docs/*
!work-docs/PLAN-*.md
!work-docs/RESEARCH-*.md
!work-docs/REVIEW-*.md
!work-docs/SPEC-*.md
!work-docs/CLOSE-*.md
!work-docs/AUDIT-*.md
# A frozen pre-change measurement is only useful if it is durable — the whole point of
# PLAN-workflow-step-audit ADR-011 is that the baseline is never recomputed, and an
# ignored file is recomputed by whoever regenerates it next.
!work-docs/BASELINE-*.md
!work-docs/RECEIPT-*.md
# Same durability argument as BASELINE-*: a pre-registered measurement and a coverage
# matrix are worthless if the next person regenerates them. ABLATION-* carries the
# pre-registration block that makes the un-gated honesty waiver survivable (ADR-007) —
# ignoring it would leave the "written BEFORE the run" claim with nothing behind it.
# MATRIX-* is the stage-2 input for the keep/retire decisions.
!work-docs/ABLATION-*.md
!work-docs/MATRIX-*.md
!work-docs/plans/

